  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1037 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nScattered high clouds across the area this evening. The upper-\nlevel pattern shows broad ridging over the western third of the\nCONUS with broad troughing over the eastern two-thirds. \nOvernight tonight, high clouds should make it more difficult \nfor fog to form, but we could still see patchy fog in low-lying \nareas and river valleys develop, burning off after sunrise. \nThursday we're looking at another quiet day with temperatures in\nthe upper 70s to low 80s for the afternoon. \n\nA weak developing low over the Nebraska Panhandle will bring a\nwarm front through eastern Nebraska Thursday night into early\nFriday. 00Z CAMs show a line of showers/storms developing along\nthat warm front near the Missouri River Valley of northeast \nNebraska into western Iowa. Soundings show sufficient elevated\ninstability, but low-level dry air will make it difficult for\nany precip to reach the ground. Good enough for a 20-30 percent\nchance of showers/storms though. \n\nIncreased warm-air advection on Friday will bring temperatures\nback up into the mid 80s. With recent trends for the NBM being a\nbit low, we could potentially approach records which are 88 for\nFriday and 91 for Saturday at Omaha (90 Friday and 91 Saturday\nfor Lincoln).\n\nSaturday we see a deepening low pressure system crossing south-\ncentral Canada push into the strong ridge over the Great Lakes\nregion, steepening the pressure gradient across the Northern\nPlains down to our area. This will really ramp up winds across \nour area with winds gusting 35-45 mph expected, strongest over\nnortheast Nebraska. These winds will be sourcing air from the\ndrier areas of New Mexico, west Texas and western Kansas. Dry,\nwindy conditions over the drier parts of central and \nnortheastern Nebraska may create fire weather concerns Saturday \nafternoon. This pattern breaks down Saturday night into Sunday\nas the Low in Canada breaks down as it gets forced overtop the\nRidge. \n\nLooking west toward the latter half of the weekend we see a deep\ntrough slide down the West Coast setting up a fairly active\nsouthwesterly flow pattern going into next week. Ridging pushing\nup out of Mexico into Texas will serve to intensify the upper-\nlevel jet spanning from southern California northeast into\nNebraska and South Dakota. This baroclinic pattern decreases\nconfidence in the fine details of the forecast for next week,\nbut ensemble guidance generally show a cold front bringing\ntemperatures back down Monday with precip chances returning \nTuesday or Wednesday. This pattern is prime for a prototypical \nFall-like severe weather event somewhere across the Great Plains\nlater next week as the deep low over southern California gets \nreabsorbed back into the upper-level flow moving in Thursday or \nFriday. Much cooler air moves in behind it. Certainly a time \nperiod to watch.",
